  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe surface cold front will exit south of the state by late \nafternoon. The shallow low level frontal circulation and lack of \nupper level forcing has maintained a dry fropa across Se Mi today. \nThe cold front has been driven southward as a 1035mb sfc high \nexpands across northern Ontario. This high will sustain low level \nanticyclonic flow across Se Mi through Tuesday as it drifts toward \nsrn Quebec, resulting in continued dry weather across Se Mi. Post \nfrontal cold air advection will drop 925mb temps down into the \nsingle digits tonight. A light gradient flow and occasional mid/high \nclouds will offer some limit to radiational cooling tonight, \nsupporting lows from the upper 30s in the thumb to mid 40s in \nDetroit. Residual shallow cold air within east-southeasterly sfc \nwinds will limit daytime mixing on Tuesday, supporting daytime highs \nof mid 50s in the thumb to low 60s across metro Detroit south.\n\nOccasional mid/high clouds will persist into Wednesday as a broad \nmid level warm air advection regime envelops the region within \nemerging deep layer southwest flow. An embedded short wave impulse is \nforecast to lift into the Great Lakes region from the southwest \nWednesday. Model cross sections indicate elevated and shallow moist \nisentropic ascent associated with this feature, not supportive of \nrain. Veering of the surface winds toward the south on Wednesday \nwith the continued eastward departure of the surface high will drive \n925mb temps into the teens. Probabilistic guidance is well clustered \non highs into the low 70s Wednesday within the pattern, roughly 15 \ndegree above seasonal norms. \n\nA northern stream short wave will drive a sfc cold front into Lower \nMi on Thursday. There are indications that the deep layer frontal \nstructure will weaken/become frontolytic as it tracks across Se Mi \nThurs/Thurs night. Nonetheless, a narrow axis of deep layer moisture \nwith some steeper mid level lapse rates will warrant a chance of \nshowers. Probabilistic guidance suggests the better chances will be \nacross the Tri Cities and thumb. Strong indications within the data \nindicate that if it were to rain, QPF amounts will be a few \nhundredths of an inch at best. Strengthening southwest flow in \nadvance of the front will set the stage for a mild day Thursday as \nmodel soundings and prob guidance support highs in the low to mid \n70s. Any semblance of post frontal cold air advection looks brief as \nthe northern stream jet retreats to the north allowing mild air to \nremain entrenched across the Great Lakes region.",
